        </div><div class="article-template__content page-width page-width--narrow rte scroll-trigger animate--slide-in">
          <div class="rocket-shopify-article lngvty-article" style="max-width:880px;margin:0 auto;color:#181818;font-family:'Avenir Next',Avenir,'Century Gothic','Trebuchet MS',sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.75;letter-spacing:.15px;">

    <p style="max-width:760px;margin:0 0 24px;color:rgba(24,24,24,.68);font-size:20px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:.2px;">
        Melasma is a hormonally driven pigmentation pattern that keeps coming back unless you manage its triggers, not just the visible colour.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:0 0 38px;padding:20px 22px;border-left:4px solid #181818;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            <strong style="font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.5px;">Quick answer:</strong>
            Melasma forms because hormones and sun exposure keep melanin production continuously elevated in a way ordinary dark spots do not. Unlike a post-acne mark that fades as skin heals, melasma returns whenever a trigger is present, so treatment has to reduce visible colour and stop new colour forming at the same time. Daily sunscreen is non-negotiable; a topical routine with kojic acid and niacinamide then does useful work on top. See a dermatologist if patches have not shifted after three months of consistent care, are spreading, or appeared suddenly without an obvious trigger.
        </p>
    </div>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        If you have spent months using a vitamin C serum on a brown patch across your cheeks and seen almost nothing change, there is a good chance you are dealing with melasma rather than ordinary hyperpigmentation. The two look similar but behave very differently, and treating them the same way usually explains why results disappoint.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        What makes melasma different
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Most dark spots on Indian skin come from inflammation. An acne lesion heals and leaves a flat brown mark behind; the skin produced extra colour as part of the wound response, and it fades as the skin turns over. This is post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation, and it responds reasonably well to brightening ingredients given enough time.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Melasma works differently. The pigment-producing cells are not reacting to an old injury. They are kept in a continuously active state by hormones, UV exposure, and in some cases heat. The cells produce melanin steadily rather than as a one-off response, which is why melasma patches are larger, more symmetrical, and sit across the cheeks, forehead, and upper lip rather than at the site of an old spot. A post-acne mark left alone fades within six to eighteen months. Melasma patches managed with no sun protection and no topical routine can stay for years, deepening whenever a trigger returns.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35642229/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#181818;text-decoration-color:#B87330;text-decoration-thickness:2px;text-underline-offset:4px;">1</a></sup>
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        What triggers it and what keeps it going
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 16px;">
        Understanding the triggers matters before you pick a product, because the wrong treatment can make melasma worse rather than better.
    </p>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Sun and daylight exposure.</strong> Even a short time outdoors without sunscreen can deepen melasma within a day or two. UV is not the only light that matters; visible light also stimulates the pigment pathway in darker skin tones, which is one reason melasma on Indian skin is harder to clear than studies on lighter-skinned populations suggest.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Hormonal changes.</strong> Pregnancy is the best-known trigger, which is why melasma is sometimes called the mask of pregnancy. Oral contraceptives and hormone replacement therapy can have the same effect. The hormonal component should be discussed with your doctor rather than managed through skincare alone.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Heat.</strong> Some people notice melasma darkens after a hot shower, exercise, or a long commute. Heat can stimulate pigment production independently of UV, which is why melasma tends to be worse in summer in India even when someone is reasonably careful about direct sun.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Irritation.</strong> Aggressive scrubs, strong chemical peels, or laser treatments used without proper assessment can cause a post-inflammatory flare on top of existing melasma. A routine that regularly irritates the skin tends to prolong pigmentation rather than clear it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        How topical ingredients work on melasma
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Kojic acid slows the enzyme that produces melanin, working on pigment at the source rather than on colour already formed. Niacinamide works later in the same process by reducing the transfer of melanin from the cells that make it to the skin cells at the surface. Using them together addresses the pathway from two directions.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35642229/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#181818;text-decoration-color:#B87330;text-decoration-thickness:2px;text-underline-offset:4px;">1</a></sup> Niacinamide also strengthens the skin barrier and reduces surface inflammation, which matters because a disrupted barrier keeps skin reactive and makes it harder to tolerate actives without flaring.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        A 2023 systematic review in the Indian Dermatology Online Journal covering topical and systemic therapies for melasma, with a focus on darker skin populations, found that combination approaches targeting more than one step of the pigment pathway consistently outperformed single-ingredient treatments, and that photoprotection is core to any melasma plan, not optional.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38099013/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#181818;text-decoration-color:#B87330;text-decoration-thickness:2px;text-underline-offset:4px;">2</a></sup>
    </p>

    <div style="margin:42px 0;padding:30px 26px;background:#181818;color:#FFFFFF;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#B87330;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">
            The LNGVTY system
        </p>
        <h2 style="margin:0 0 12px;color:#FFFFFF;font-size:31px;line-height:1.15;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.8px;">
            Choose by skin concern
        </h2>
        <p style="margin:0 0 24px;color:rgba(255,255,255,.7);font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;">
            For uneven tone and lingering pigment, a serum that combines kojic acid, niacinamide, and barrier support in one step keeps the routine simple and lowers the risk of irritation.
        </p>

