    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Building a routine that does not make things worse
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        The most important addition to a melasma routine is sunscreen, worn every morning, reapplied if you are outdoors. No topical brightener can outpace daily UV resetting the pigment pathway. Once that is covered, an active serum can start doing useful work.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        A melasma routine works best when it is short. More actives mean more chances for irritation, and irritation deepens pigment. The routine that tends to work is one active serum on clean, damp skin, a moisturiser, and sunscreen each morning. The <a href="/products/dark-spots-pore-control-serum-30ml" style="color:#181818;text-decoration-color:#B87330;text-decoration-thickness:2px;text-underline-offset:4px;">Dark Spots, Pore Control Serum</a> combines kojic acid and niacinamide with five ceramides and Centella asiatica in a single step. Directions: cleanse, apply 3 to 4 drops on damp skin, moisturise, and use sunscreen in the morning. It is designed as the only active step, so extra retinol or brightening acids are not needed alongside it. In the serum's own clinical study, 100% of patients improved dark spots in 8 weeks with twice-daily use in 36 Indian women aged 24 to 44; results may vary.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:34px 0;padding:24px 26px;border-top:1px solid #B87330;border-bottom:1px solid #B87330;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 7px;color:#B87330;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Keep in mind</p>
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            Melasma responds slowly, and visible improvement usually takes at least eight to twelve weeks of consistent use. If patches are spreading, become raised, or appeared very suddenly, see a dermatologist before starting any active ingredients.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Melasma versus post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation at a glance
    </h2>

    <div style="margin:24px 0 34px;overflow-x:auto;border-top:2px solid #181818;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);">
        <table style="width:100%;min-width:620px;border-collapse:collapse;text-align:left;font-size:15px;line-height:1.55;">
            <thead>
                <tr style="background:#FFFEF3;">
                    <th style="padding:15px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);font-size:12px;letter-spacing:1.2px;text-transform:uppercase;"></th>
                    <th style="padding:15px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);font-size:12px;letter-spacing:1.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Melasma</th>
                    <th style="padding:15px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);font-size:12px;letter-spacing:1.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);font-weight:700;">Cause</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Hormones, UV exposure, heat, genetics</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Inflammation from acne, eczema, or a wound</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);font-weight:700;">Pattern</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Symmetrical patches on cheeks, forehead, upper lip</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Flat marks at the site of healed breakouts or injury</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);font-weight:700;">Without treatment</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Persists; deepens with sun or hormonal change</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Gradually fades as skin turns over (6 to 18 months)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;font-weight:700;">Treatment priority</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;">Trigger control first, then topical brightening</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;">Topical brightening; anti-inflammatory care for active breakouts</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Timelines and maintenance
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Melasma improvement is measured in months. The first change you are likely to notice is that patches stop deepening and the edges look less sharp. Visible lightening usually comes between eight and sixteen weeks of consistent topical use and good sun protection; deep pigment takes longer, and topical treatment alone may not be enough. Finishing a course of treatment does not reset the skin permanently. Warm months, a contraceptive change, or a run of skipped sunscreen can bring patches back. A 2019 systematic review of randomised controlled trials on topical melasma treatments found that recurrence is common and that photoprotection is central to maintaining any improvement.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31741361/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#181818;text-decoration-color:#B87330;text-decoration-thickness:2px;text-underline-offset:4px;">3</a></sup>
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        When to see a dermatologist
    </h2>

    <p style="margin:0 0 16px;">
        Melasma responds to topical management for many people, but some situations call for a dermatologist rather than another serum:
    </p>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>No change after three months.</strong> If patches look exactly the same after three months of consistent sunscreen and topical care, it is worth checking whether the diagnosis is correct and whether deeper or hormonal melasma needs a different approach.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Spreading or new patches.</strong> Melasma spreading despite sun protection may need a systemic option such as oral tranexamic acid, which a dermatologist can assess and prescribe.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Sudden or unusual onset.</strong> A pigmentation pattern that appeared very quickly, is raised or itchy, or sits outside the typical cheek and forehead distribution should be assessed before treating at home.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Hormonal treatment involved.</strong> If melasma appeared or worsened after a change in contraceptive or during or after pregnancy, a doctor should be part of the conversation.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin:56px 0 18px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Frequently asked questions
    </h2>
    <div style="border-top:2px solid #181818;">
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can melasma go away on its own?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">It can fade when triggers are absent, such as after pregnancy ends or when hormonal contraceptives are changed, but it rarely clears completely without consistent sun protection and topical treatment. For most people it is a long-term condition to manage rather than a short-term one to cure.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Is melasma the same as sun damage?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">No, though sun exposure worsens both. Sun damage produces scattered spots from cumulative UV over years. Melasma produces larger, more uniform patches driven by hormonal sensitivity and UV together, and it typically appears symmetrically on both sides of the face.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Does vitamin C clear melasma?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Vitamin C supports a more even-looking tone by reducing oxidative stress at the surface, and it is a useful part of a broader routine. On its own it is not strong enough to clear established melasma, particularly deeper patches. It works better alongside ingredients that slow melanin production at the source, such as kojic acid.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Why does melasma come back after treatment?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Topical treatment reduces visible pigment but does not permanently change the skin's sensitivity to its triggers. Sun exposure, hormonal shifts, or heat can reactivate the pathway. Staying on a maintenance routine with consistent sunscreen is what keeps improvement from being temporary.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can I use a brightening serum while pregnant?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Pregnancy itself can cause melasma, but ingredient choices during pregnancy should be discussed with your doctor first. Check with your doctor if you are pregnant or breastfeeding before adding any new active serum.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Will a laser treatment clear melasma?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Laser and light-based treatments have mixed results for melasma. Some approaches that work well for post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation can cause a pigment flare in melasma-prone skin. A dermatologist should assess the type and depth of pigment before any procedural treatment.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">How long does topical treatment take to work?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Most people notice that patches stop deepening within four to six weeks. Visible lightening usually takes eight to sixteen weeks or longer, depending on how deep the pigment is and how consistently sunscreen is used alongside the serum.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Is melasma more common in India?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">It is more commonly reported in India and other South Asian countries. Darker skin tones are more prone to visible pigmentation changes from hormonal shifts or sun exposure, and the climate means higher average UV exposure year-round. The condition affects all skin types but tends to be more persistent in people with darker complexions.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        References
    </h2>
